## Migration: Thread.Abort → Cooperative Cancellation (.NET 8)

### Why this breaks

`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 6+. `Thread.ResetAbort()` is also gone. You must switch to **cooperative cancellation** via `CancellationToken`.

### Pattern Classification

| Usage | Pattern | Replacement |
|-------|---------|-------------|
| `_workerThread.Abort()` | Cancellable work loop | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Control flow cleanup | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Continue after abort | Check `token.IsCancellationRequested` |
| `Thread.Sleep(2000)` in Stop | Blocking wait | `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne` or `Join(timeout)` |

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock waits
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Clean up partial work — same logic that was in the
                // ThreadAbortException catch block
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort needed — we simply continue to loop exit
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Give the thread time to finish gracefully
        _workerThread?.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2));

        _cts.Dispose();
        _cts = null;
    }
}
```

### Key changes to `GetNextItem`

If `GetNextItem` blocks (e.g., on a queue), it **must** accept and respect the token — otherwise cancellation stalls:

```csharp
// Before: blocks indefinitely
private WorkItem GetNextItem() => _queue.Take();

// After: throws OperationCanceledException when cancelled
private WorkItem GetNextItem(CancellationToken token) => _queue.Take(token);
// BlockingCollection<T>.Take(CancellationToken) supports this natively
```

### Critical Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Impact |
|---------|--------|
| **Not passing token to blocking calls** | `Stop()` hangs forever — the thread never wakes up to check cancellation |
| **Catching `OperationCanceledException` too broadly** | Use `when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` to avoid swallowing unrelated exceptions |
| **Forgetting to dispose `CancellationTokenSource`** | Leaks timers/callbacks. Always dispose in `Stop()` or via `using` |
| **Assuming cancellation is instant** | It only fires at the next checkpoint. Add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` inside long `ProcessItem` work |
| **Replacing `Thread` with `Task.Run` without thought** | `Task.Run` uses the thread pool — fine here, but if `ProcessLoop` is long-lived, a dedicated thread (as shown) avoids pool starvation |

### If `GetNextItem` can't be modified

If you cannot change the blocking call to accept a token (e.g., third-party code), use a timeout loop:

```csharp
while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
{
    // Poll with short timeout instead of blocking forever
    if (_queue.TryTake(out var item, TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(500)))
    {
        ProcessItem(item, token);
    }
}
```

This trades a small polling delay for guaranteed cancellation responsiveness.
